250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (Water treatment plants decide which coagulant to use largely through empirical testing and institutional experience - not through any deep understanding of why one coagulant binds to one pollutant better than another. Zainab's project asks whether that gap can be closed at the molecular level: can computational descriptors of how a coagulant and a pollutant physically interact actually predict how well coagulation will work in practice?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (Working with natural coagulants - chitosan, tannic acid, moringa - against a representative set of pollutants including dyes, PFAS \("forever chemicals"\), and natural organic matter, she built a computational pipeline starting with molecular docking and binding-energy calculations using molecular mechanics. As the project progressed, the limits of relying on docking scores alone became apparent, pushing the work toward more rigorous energy calculations that account for molecular charge, solvent effects, protonation state, and electronic structure - the kind of detail that separates a rough correlation from a real mechanistic explanation.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
