276 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 121 0 R
/T (Article 6.4 of the Paris Agreement establishes the international mechanism for greenhouse gas mitigation and sustainable development - but the guidance governing it is scattered across CMA decisions, Supervisory Body rulings, and technical annexes, written in dense legal language, updated frequently, and hard to search. For governments, project developers, and civil society groups engaging with Article 6.4 for the first time - particularly from developing countries and under-resourced institutions - that fragmentation itself becomes a barrier to participation.)
/E (Article 6.4 of the Paris Agreement establishes the international mechanism for greenhouse gas mitigation and sustainable development - but the guidance governing it is scattered across CMA decisions, Supervisory Body rulings, and technical annexes, written in dense legal language, updated frequently, and hard to search. For governments, project developers, and civil society groups engaging with Article 6.4 for the first time - particularly from developing countries and under-resourced institutions - that fragmentation itself becomes a barrier to participation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 121 0 R
/T (Grace's work during this internship was building the answer to that problem: the Article 6.4 Navigator, a retrieval-augmented generation \(RAG\) system that lets any stakeholder ask a plain-language questions - and receive an answer grounded entirely in official UNFCCC text, with citations back to the exact source document. Under the hood, the system retrieves relevant passages from the official Article 6.4 corpus, uses a language model to draft a plain-language answer from that retrieved context, and returns the answer alongside direct references - so the tool never speculates or substitutes for the official guidance, it simply makes that guidance reachable. Multilingual support aligned with the UN's official languages was built into the design from the outset, since much of Article 6.4's intended audience works in French, Spanish, and Arabic as often as English. Alongside the Navigator, Grace also co-developed the PACM podcast through to its launch and helped design the Technical Expert Review Course from the ground up.)
/E (Grace's work during this internship was building the answer to that problem: the Article 6.4 Navigator, a retrieval-augmented generation \(RAG\) system that lets any stakeholder ask a plain-language questions - and receive an answer grounded entirely in official UNFCCC text, with citations back to the exact source document. Under the hood, the system retrieves relevant passages from the official Article 6.4 corpus, uses a language model to draft a plain-language answer from that retrieved context, and returns the answer alongside direct references - so the tool never speculates or substitutes for the official guidance, it simply makes that guidance reachable. Multilingual support aligned with the UN's official languages was built into the design from the outset, since much of Article 6.4's intended audience works in French, Spanish, and Arabic as often as English. Alongside the Navigator, Grace also co-developed the PACM podcast through to its launch and helped design the Technical Expert Review Course from the ground up.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 121 0 R
/T ("Capacity building is not about holding conferences and workshops," Grace reflects. "It is about what people can use without your presence - the tools, guidance, and resources you leave with them, that they can pick up easily in their day-to-day work long after any event is over." Policies are only as strong as the practitioners who can actually implement them - and most of the world's climate guidance is dense, technical, and scattered across formal decision documents. Work that turns that guidance into something a person can actually use is what makes international climate policy real on the ground rather than theoretical on paper.  )
/E ("Capacity building is not about holding conferences and workshops," Grace reflects. "It is about what people can use without your presence - the tools, guidance, and resources you leave with them, that they can pick up easily in their day-to-day work long after any event is over." Policies are only as strong as the practitioners who can actually implement them - and most of the world's climate guidance is dense, technical, and scattered across formal decision documents. Work that turns that guidance into something a person can actually use is what makes international climate policy real on the ground rather than theoretical on paper.  )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
