/Title (Search-and-rescue robotics doesn't leave much room for theory alone - the environments are rough, unpredictable, and often hazardous by design. Rhoda spent a week at ETH Zurich's RobotX summer school getting exactly that kind of hands-on exposure: lectures and tutorials on core robotics concepts, paired with programming autonomous ground robots built for rough terrain and testing them across varied environments.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1812 0 R
/Next 1823 0 R
>>
endobj
1823 0 obj
<<
/Title (The week ran alongside ARCHE \(Advanced Robotic Capabilities for Hazardous Environments\), which brought legged robots, teleoperated excavators, amphibious vehicles, and UAVs into simulated disaster scenarios - giving participants a front-row view of how different robotic platforms are designed to complement each other when a single robot type isn't enough for the job.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1812 0 R
/Prev 1822 0 R
>>
endobj
1824 0 obj
<<
/Title (Programs like RobotX exist to put students in front of that complexity early, with real hardware instead of only simulations, so that when disaster-response robotics needs a new generation of researchers, some of them will already know what the field actually looks like up close.)
